# Rails Concerns

Concerns define reusable behavior. They must stay small and must not silently change the including
class.

## Enforcement

`included do` and `prepended do` are rejected by the `Umaxica/NoConcernInclusionHooks` cop, and
implicitly public methods by `Umaxica/ExplicitMethodVisibility`. Existing violations are baselined
and may only decrease. See `docs/architecture/method-visibility-and-concerns.md`.

## Keep concerns low-side-effect

Including a concern must not, on its own:

- install callbacks
- expose helpers
- mutate sessions, cookies, or other state
- change authorization
- trigger other runtime side effects

unless an existing, reviewed pattern explicitly requires it.

## Make side effects opt-in

The including controller, model, or service should opt into any side effect explicitly. A reader of
the including class should be able to see what the concern activates, not have it happen by the mere
act of inclusion.

## Visibility

Every method needs an explicit `public`, `protected` or `private` declaration; Ruby's implicit
public default is not accepted. Keep concern methods private by default. Expose a public method only
when the including class or an external caller needs a stable public contract, and make that
boundary intentional.

## Contract with the including class

A concern states the methods it needs from its includer by fixed name in its header comment, and
calls them directly. It never probes with `respond_to?` and falls back to other behavior. Keep HTTP
concerns (input validation, audit attributes, response shaping) apart from model concerns
(invariants, state transitions); one concern does not do both. A concern shared across the `app`,
`com`, and `org` surfaces must not switch behavior on surface flags.

For authorization-bearing controllers, the concrete controller names the target class, how the
record is loaded, the policy and rule passed to `authorize!`, the Step-Up scope, and the operation it
calls. A concern may shape the page or validate input around those calls; it does not make them.
